  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nQuiet weather will come to an end by later today in response to\nan encroaching cold front. Until then, another unseasonably warm\nday is on tap for this afternoon as the forecast area becomes\nplanted in the warm sector of the advancing front and parent\nlow pressure system. While most afternoon high temperatures will\nfall well short of daily records today, Elkins and Beckley will\ncome close to matching or slightly exceeding their record highs\nof 82 degrees.\n\nOnset timing of showers and storms ahead of the cold front is\nslated to reach our far northwest zones as soon as midday, with\nintensification of convection progged in the afternoon as the\nfront slowly sags southward into the forecast area. Hi-res model\nguidance hints at scattered activity pressing further down into\nthe area through the course of the afternoon and this evening.\nTapping into the warm and unstable environment planted ahead of\nthe frontal passage, a few strong to potentially severe storms\ncould transpire and impose damaging wind gusts. Timeframe of the\nstrongest storm potential will be strongly aligned to daytime\nheating, with convective activity diminishing in intensity after\nsunset this evening.\n\nAdditionally, locally heavy downpours attached to precipitation\nahead and along the front will yield high water concerns. While\nsevere concerns peter out this evening, moderate to heavy rain\npotential will prevail into the overnight hours. The orientation\nof the passing front will also be key to flooding concerns\ntonight, as repetitive rounds of rain moving from west to east\nalong the boundary could fall over locations that will grow more\nsusceptible to rising creeks and streams. The latest excessive \nrainfall outlook has upgraded portions of Kentucky, Ohio, and \nWest Virginia into a slight risk (Level 2) for flash flooding \nconcerns through Thursday morning as a result.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe frontal boundary will continue to loiter overhead or just\nslightly south of the area on Thursday, with additional rounds\nof showers and embedded thunderstorms throughout the morning.\nForecast trends retain the notion of the boundary retreating\nnorthward as a warm front by midday on Thursday, and once again\nencouraging onshore flow and slight warm air advection heading\ninto Friday.\n\nSurface low pressure strengthening off the lee of the Rockies\nwill then infiltrate the Ohio Valley for the end of the work\nweek. A strong cold front attached to this feature is progged \nto push through the region on Friday, with a line of gusty showers\nbarreling into the forecast area in the afternoon. Behind this \nfront, a deepening H5 trough will invoke a tightening pressure\ngradient that will set up elevated surface gusts across the area\nfor Friday evening. Gusts on the upwards of 25kts across the\nlowlands could be observed in the wake of the FROPA, and nearing\nwind advisory thresholds of 40kts along the higher ridges,\nespecially early Saturday morning.\n\nA blast of arctic air infiltrates the Central Appalachians\nheading into this weekend, with a stark change in morning low\ntemperatures from Friday to Saturday mornings. Sub-freezing\ntemperatures arriving to the higher terrain early Saturday\nmorning could also promote the first snowflakes of the season as\nprecipitation wraps up behind the front. Global models aren't\ntoo keen on accumulations with this small window of wintry\nweather, but a light dusting isn't out of the question for the\nhighest ridges of our mountain zones.\n\nHigh pressure settles into the area for this weekend with dry\nweather in tow. Strong radiational cooling nights will promote \nsub-freezing temperatures for the vast majority of the area \nSunday morning before a moderating trend takes hold early next \nweek.",
